The coordination agent plans; written steps execute; the agent is called again only on deviation. Between deviations no AI runs at all, which is what makes this affordable.

Store the chain under `refs/karr-foundation/chain/*` and run logs under `refs/karr-foundation/log/<date>-<id>`, plus retention (`log clean`) so the namespace does not grow without bound.

A step names no agent — that would tie shared state to one machine. It carries id, kind (ticket | shell | question | plan), repo, timeout, `needs` (step ids), a `precheck`, and `on_*` policies.

The chain is a DAG: steps without a `needs` edge may run concurrently, so the planner leaves edges out rather than serialising by hand.

A step whose `precheck` no longer holds is not executed — it is marked stale and calls the planner. That is what stops an out-of-date chain from doing damage; it costs time, not correctness.

Design: `karr get-refs refs/karr-foundation/spec/fleet-execution.md`
Implemented as App::karr::Foundation::ChainStore (new module) plus a write-side guard in Git::validate_helper_ref. Commit f2f7d27, local only, not pushed.

LAYOUT: refs/karr-foundation/chain/meta (header: id, created, limits passed through untouched) + refs/karr-foundation/chain/step/<id> (one ref per step, YAML) + refs/karr-foundation/log/<date>-<id> (JSON lines, segmented +000001 at 8 KiB like ActivityLog since #171). One ref per step is what makes a per-step CAS possible; the header is written LAST and is the commit point, because ready_steps only considers steps whose chain id matches it, so a half-finished replacement is a window in which nothing is ready rather than one in which the wrong thing runs.

STEP SCHEMA: id, kind (ticket|shell|question|plan), repo, ticket, needs, timeout, precheck, on_* policies, plus command/note/state bookkeeping. 'agent' (and 'agents') is REFUSED, not ignored -- that is the epic's dividing line enforced at the cheapest place. on_* values are free-form: the vocabulary belongs to the runner (#186). Unknown keys warn like Agents.pm does.

DAG: ready_steps = pending steps whose needs are all done; no needs edge means concurrent. Cycles, unknown needs, self-edges and unreadable prechecks are refused at write_chain, and nothing is written when they are. Replacing a chain that still has a running step needs force.

PRECHECK: grammar lives here (parse_precheck: <fact> ==|!= <value>), facts are supplied by the caller because measuring one means reading a board = execution. A missing/unknown fact makes the precheck NOT hold, for both operators -- uncertainty always falls to the stale side. mark_stale records state/reason/stale_at; calling the planner is #186's move.

CONCURRENCY: update_step uses read_ref_with_oid + write_ref_cas inside retry_contended -- the board's machinery, no second one. Test forks 12 processes racing one claim: exactly 1 wins, 11 decline, none die.

RETENTION: prune_logs drops runs older than keep_days (14) and everything past the newest keep_runs (500); count is the knob that actually bounds the namespace. It runs automatically when a run log is OPENED (auto_prune), once per run.

NAMESPACE DECISION (asked for explicitly): refs/karr-foundation stays reachable through set-refs/get-refs as a whole -- this epic's own spec lives at refs/karr-foundation/spec/fleet-execution.md and was written with set-refs -- but chain/ and log/ are now WRITE-blocked while reads stay open. validate_helper_ref gained an optional for_write => 1 which only Cmd::SetRefs passes; get-refs does not. Reason: set-refs writes last-writer-wins and joins argv with a space, so hand-writing a step either clobbers one a run just claimed or lands as a payload that no longer parses, and the stale-precheck guard protects against an out-of-date chain, not a malformed one.

DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT: no CLI at all. No 'karr chain' command, no 'karr-foundation --log-clean' -- an explicit log clean would need a hub-resolving config key that belongs to karr-foundation/#190, and retention that only runs when someone types it bounds nothing, which is why it hangs off opening a run log instead. No chain execution (#186), no sync of this namespace (#190; prune deletes leave no tombstone because Git::delete_ref only writes those for refs/karr/*, so remote deletion is #190's), no question mailbox (#191). Nothing loads ChainStore yet, which is why it is listed in t/00-load.t.

